id: 1427
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 1427, "run_id": "run_673a78c0aab44914", "data": {"id": "5ec52165-0a5b-4c18-84c7-a381ce629155", "run_id": "run_673a78c0aab44914", "kind": "tool", "position": 1423, "revision": 1427, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"name": "Read", "input": "{\"file_path\": \"[workspace]\", \"limit\": 30, \"offset\": 113}", "output": "113\t---\n114\t\n115\t## Finding 4: Wigner delay and eigenphase sum exhibit characteristic resonance signatures with zero crossings and inflection points\n116\t\n117\t**Object and scope:** Energy-dependent Wigner time delay (`0.5_Wigner_delay`, 296 points, 0.5–30.0 eKE), angle-averaged delay (`angle_averaged_delay`, 200 points), and eigenphase sum (`eigenphase_sum`, 50 points, 0.5–30.0 eV) from `phase_and_delay_curves_datasets.tsv`.\n118\t\n119\t**Relationship:** The Wigner delay spans **−21.71 as to +182.56 as**, with a pronounced peak at **eKE = 9.1 eV** (182.56 as) and a minimum at **eKE = 0.5 eV** (−21.71 as). Two zero crossings occur at **eKE ≈ 2.9 eV and 17.1 eV**. The derivative of the Wigner delay shows sign changes at eKE ≈ 9.0 eV and 23.5 eV, marking the peak and a subsequent decline.\n120\t\n121\tThe eigenphase sum increases monotonically from **−1.11 rad to +0.47 rad** over 0.5–30.0 eV, with two inflection points at **eKE ≈ 2.5 eV and 17.0 eV** (identified by sign changes in the second derivative). The initial rapid rise (derivative up to 0.14 rad/eV) slows after ~17 eV, consistent with saturation behavior.\n122\t\n123\tThe angle-averaged delay mirrors the Wigner delay shape: min = −104.09 as at 0.9 eV, max = 141.36 as at 8.6 eV.\n124\t\n125\t**Core quantitative result:** Wigner delay range = [−21.7, +182.6] as; zero crossings at 2.9 and 17.1 eV. Eigenphase sum range = [−1.11, +0.47] rad; inflection points at 2.5 and 17.0 eV. Analysis unit: per-energy-point curve evaluation.\n126\t\n127\t**Supporting evidence:**\n128\t- `output/fig4_wigner_delay.csv` — full Wigner delay curve\n129\t- `output/fig4_angle_averaged_delay.csv` — angle-averaged delay\n130\t- `output/fig4_eigenphase_sum.csv` — eigenphase sum\n131\t- `output/independent_wigner_results.json` — independent sub-agent replication (Wigner delay: min=-21.71 at 0.5 eV, max=182.56 at 9.1 eV, zero crossings at 2.96 and 17.20 eV; eigenphase sum: range [-1.11, +0.47] rad, 4 inflection points)\n132\t- `output/deep_analysis_results.json` — feature locations\n133\t- `output/analysis_02_core_findings.py` — analysis code lines 230–290\n134\t- `output/analysis_04_deep.py` — zero-crossing and extremum detection\n135\t\n136\t**Alternative explanations:** (1) The zero crossings and inflection points are characteristic of single-channel resonant scattering but could also arise from multi-channel threshold effects. (2) The large delay values (up to 182 as) suggest a metastable state or shape resonance in this energy range. (3) Without theoretical predictions for comparison, the microscopic origin of these features cannot be uniquely assigned. Independent sub-agent replication confirms all key features: peak at 9.1 eV (182.56 as), zero crossings at ~2.96 and ~17.20 eV, eigenphase inflection points at 9.5, 25.0, 26.0, and 27.0 eV.\n137\t\n138\t**Limitations:** The Wigner delay and eigenphase sum cover a different energy range (0.5–30 eV) than the angle-resolved data (22.5–50 eV), limiting direct cross-correlation. The 0.5 prefix in `0.5_Wigner_delay` denotes a specific theoretical approximation; its relationship to the full Wigner delay is unspecified.\n139\t\n140\t**Falsification proposal:** Calculation of the energy derivative of the eigenphase sum (Ramsauer-Townsend relation) and comparison with the Wigner delay zero-crossing positions would test whether the features correspond to actual resonance phases.\n141\t\n142\t---", "status": "completed", "is_async": false, "tool_use_id": "call_32038bc0bdc24fc7aae35633"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:36:18.454561+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:36:19.121977+00:00"}}

