id: 1543
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 1543, "run_id": "run_673a78c0aab44914", "data": {"id": "5e45114a-ed00-46cb-a06c-d7d6f72167a8", "run_id": "run_673a78c0aab44914", "kind": "tool", "position": 1539, "revision": 1543, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"name": "Read", "input": "{\"file_path\": \"[workspace]\", \"limit\": 30, \"offset\": 27}", "output": "27\t## Finding 1: Experimental-theory phase disagreement is large (~4.08 rad) and increases with photon energy after proper grid alignment; magnitude agreement remains tight\n28\t\n29\t**Object and scope:** Comparison of experimental vs. theoretical unwrapped phase and magnitude across the overlapping energy range (~23.2–41.8 eV), spanning 99 theory energy points and 181 emission angles (0–180°).\n30\t\n31\t**Grid alignment declaration:** The experiment grid (276 points, 0.1 eV step, 22.5–50.0 eV) and theory grid (99 points, irregular steps, 23.249–41.839 eV) share only one exact common energy (40.1 eV). Following DATA_GUIDE.md §30, cubic spline interpolation (`scipy.interpolate.CubicSpline`) was used to map the experiment angle-resolved phase/magnitude/delay grids onto the theory energy grid. Perturbation uncertainty was quantified via 100 realizations of ±1% Gaussian noise on interpolated values.\n32\t\n33\t**CRITICAL CORRECTION (Round 2):** An initial index-based alignment (pairing row i of experiment with row i of theory) produced systematically biased results because it compared different physical energies: at idx=0, exp@22.5 eV was paired with th@23.249 eV (Δ=0.75 eV); at idx=98, exp@32.3 eV was paired with th@41.839 eV (Δ=9.54 eV). The mismatch grew linearly at 0.093 eV/index (r²=0.9996). This flawed pairing produced an underestimated slope of 0.090 rad/eV and mean diff of 2.54 rad. The corrected spline-aligned analysis replaces these values.\n34\t\n35\t**Relationship (corrected):** After proper energy-grid alignment, the mean absolute difference between experimental and theoretical phase (angle-averaged) is **4.08 rad** (min 3.48, max 4.43 rad). The phase difference increases with photon energy at a rate of **0.046 rad/eV** (r² = 0.60, p < 10⁻²⁰). The perturbation analysis confirms robustness: slope CI [0.0456, 0.0459] rad/eV, mean diff CI [4.078, 4.080] rad. In contrast, the magnitude agreement is much tighter: mean |exp − th| magnitude difference is only **0.127** (max 0.165).\n36\t\n37\t**Angle-resolved pattern:** The disagreement is strongly angle-dependent. After proper energy-grid alignment, the mean phase difference increases monotonically from ~3.48 rad at 24.8 eV to ~4.43 rad at 35.7 eV before declining slightly. The perturbation analysis confirms this trend is robust: slope CI [0.0456, 0.0459] rad/eV, mean diff CI [4.078, 4.080] rad. At low energies (<28 eV), phases agree best near 90° (mean |diff| ≈ 0.09 rad) but diverge sharply at wide angles 81–89° and 91–98° (up to 6.6 rad). At mid energies (28–35 eV), disagreement is more uniform across most angles (2.6–5.3 rad) except near 90° (~0.20 rad) and wide angles (~6.5 rad). This non-uniform angular structure suggests the discrepancy reflects missing physics in the theory model at specific angle-energy combinations, not a simple global offset. The angle-resolved data was re-computed on the spline-aligned grid using `working/analysis_round3_angle_resolved.py`; results are saved to `output/fig1_angle_resolved_aligned.csv`.\n38\t\n39\t**Dual-口径 clarification:** Two computation orders exist in the pipeline: (a) \"angle-average then absolute difference\" (used in Finding 1 text and `analysis_fix_energy_alignment.py`): mean(|mean_angle(exp) − mean_angle(th)|) = 4.08 rad; (b) \"absolute difference per cell then average\" (used in `analysis_03_figures.py`): mean(mean_cell(|exp − th|)) = 4.01 rad. Both yield consistent results under the old index-alignment. Under the new spline-aligned grid, definition (a) gives 4.08 rad (from `aligned_exp_theory_comparison.json`); definition (b) has not been re-executed on the spline-aligned grid — the value 4.01 rad is from the pre-correction analysis. The earlier JSON file `analysis_results.json` contained the pre-correction value 4.0137 (old alignment, cell-averaged口径 b); this has been superseded by `aligned_exp_theory_comparison.json`.\n40\t\n41\t**Core quantitative result:** Phase mean diff = 4.08 rad (spline-aligned); slope = 0.046 rad/eV, r² = 0.60, p < 10⁻²⁰. Magnitude mean diff = 0.127. Perturbation CI: slope [0.0456, 0.0459], mean [4.078, 4.080]. Analysis unit: per-energy, angle-averaged comparison over 99 theory energy indices.\n42\t\n43\t**Supporting evidence:**\n44\t- `output/fig1_aligned_phase_diff.csv` — per-energy phase difference values (spline-aligned)\n45\t- `output/aligned_exp_theory_comparison.json` — grid characterization, old-alignment diagnosis, perturbation analysis, aligned comparison statistics\n46\t- `output/analysis_fix_energy_alignment.py` — full alignment code\n47\t- `output/analysis_02_core_findings.py` — original (flawed) analysis code, lines 30–80\n48\t- `output/analysis_04_deep.py` — angle-resolved breakdown by energy region\n49\t\n50\t**Robustness:** The linear trend persists across all angle subsets. When restricted to the best-agreement angle (90°), the slope remains positive (0.045 rad/eV, r² = 0.72). The perturbation analysis (100 realizations of ±1% Gaussian noise) confirms robustness: slope CI [0.0456, 0.0459] rad/eV, mean diff CI [4.078, 4.080] rad — the perturbation-induced variation is <0.1% of the measured effect. The magnitude-phase asymmetry (tight magnitude, loose phase) is consistent across all angles and energies. Independent sub-agent replication confirmed Findings 2/3/4 (angular coefficients, DME trends, Wigner features); the exp-vs-theory phase alignment (Finding 1) was not independently replicated by a sub-agent.\n51\t\n52\t**Alternative explanations:** (1) The phase wrapping/unwrapping procedure may introduce systematic errors that accumulate with energy. (2) The theory model may use approximations (e.g., single-channel, neglect of certain correlation effects) that become increasingly inaccurate at higher photon energies. (3) The spline-derived nature of the experimental grid means correlated errors could amplify apparent differences. These cannot be distinguished with the current data alone.\n53\t\n54\t**Limitations:** Only one exact common energy (40.1 eV) exists between grids; interpolation is necessary. The dense grid cells are not independent replicates. No error bars are available for individual phase values. The perturbation analysis uses ±1% noise as a proxy for interpolation uncertainty; the true uncertainty may differ.\n55\t\n56\t**Falsification proposal:** Independent measurement of the unwrapped phase at 3–5 additional energies within 35–42 eV using a different spectroscopic technique would test whether the monotonic increase continues or saturates.", "status": "completed", "is_async": false, "tool_use_id": "call_33a2e0a7f41c43239284e1a9"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:37:59.847133+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:38:00.743550+00:00"}}

